{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310783,"nonce":"5HcS/LPqlMIkrHpFVJPg+q35Eg4=","title":"Cranelift SDOT fires (has_dotprod auto + tree-shape fix); nue Haxe Q4/Q6 matmul now COHERENT on cranelift-default — the \\\"cranelift ...","message":"**The \"cranelift-only non-SIMD miscompile\" was WRONG.** Every primitive is bit-correct on cranelift (disasm-verified: sdot fires in subDotVec; SIMD16i8.get, f16ToF32/pow2, SIMD4i32 make/mul/add/splat/sum all match LLVM; standalone q4DotMA4 matches LLVM EXACTLY). The garbage had TWO real causes, both now FIXED (uncommitted as of this session — pending user commit decision):\n\n1. **Output write corruption (all tiers, not cranelift-specific).** Q4Matmul wrote results via `y.data():Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e` + `slot.write(sum)`. `Float`=f64 but the tensor is F32 → disasm showed `lsl w,#3; str d` = 8-byte f64 store at 8-byte stride into a 4-byte buffer → corruption. A standalone Tensor-write repro (`/tmp/twr2.hx`) is broken on BOTH cranelift AND llvm — so the earlier \"--llvm → Paris\" was the FFI fallback, NOT proof the Haxe kernel worked on llvm. FIX: added dtype-aware `Tensor.setFlat(i, v)` (the missing counterpart to getFlat) — runtime `rayzor_tensor_set_flat` narrows via `store_f32_at(dtype)`. Files: runtime/src/tensor.rs, plugin_impl.rs (register), compiler/src/stdlib/tensor.rs (wrapper+extern), runtime_mapping.rs (`setFlat`=\u003e`Tensor_set_flat`), haxe-std Tensor.hx. Q4Matmul.hx now `y.setFlat(ob+n, sum)`. After this, Q4 matmuls match FFI to ~1e-6 (f32 rounding).\n\n2. **Q4Matmul was Q4_K-only; Q4_K_M models mix in Q6_K.** attn_v / ffn_down are promoted to Q6_K (210-byte blocks, QSCHEME_Q6_K=2). Decoding them as Q4_K (144-byte) gave garbage scales (d=7124) → inf cascade. FIX: wrote pure-Haxe `q6DotMA4` (mirrors runtime-core `vec_dot_q6_K_q8_K` SDOT path: ql low-nibble + qh 2-bit pair → 6-bit weight 0..63, SDOT vs shared Q8_K activation, −32 bias folded into 32·Σx via bsums), added `Q6_K` to the QScheme enum (ordinal 2), and dispatch in Q4Matmul.matmul on `qw.scheme()` (blockBytes 144 vs 210; activation side is scheme-agnostic). q6 output matches FFI to ~0.001–0.01 (integer-SDOT vs the native FFI's f32-dequant path — expected divergence, not a bug). Note memory bugs_q6k_sdot_no_win: Q6_K SDOT IS a proven win in the RUST kernel with 2-row tiling; don't recite it as \"no win\".\n\n**RESULT: `RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL=1` (no requant flag) → \"The capital of France is Paris.\" on cranelift DEFAULT tier** with the pure-Haxe kernel handling both Q4_K and Q6_K. Suite 156/156; fmt+clippy clean.\n\n**SDOT on cranelift — FIXED, committed d9413dd5** (VectorDot builds the exact FEAT_DotProd ISLE tree: widen each i16 product to i32 SEPARATELY then pairwise-reduce → single `sdot`). **SIMD @:native wrapper cross-module linking — FIXED, committed c6a5c13f** (IrModule::link_selfcontained_wrapper_stubs copies real bodies over empty `unreachable` stubs).\n\n**SLOWNESS — measured with `sample` (macOS), NOT a codegen-tier issue.** Forcing `--preset benchmark --tier-promotion true` (max cranelift, still no LLVM) did NOTHING (1.10→1.14 tok/s) → it's the codegen STRUCTURE, not opt level/regalloc. Profile of a live 60-tok run (resolve JIT addrs via /tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.\u003cpid\u003e.csv): hot = `\u003clambda_0\u003e`/`\u003clambda_1\u003e` (the matmul closures) + system-lib 0x18bb… (pthread) ~17%. **4842 distinct threads** — `WorkerPool.parallelRows` does `Thread.spawn` per node PER matmul call (WorkerPool.hx ~131), ~690 spawns/token. The persistent spin-wait pool (4001079) is RUST/FFI-only; the Haxe path never got one.\n\n**CORRECTED (workflow wf_ff056a3c, adversarially verified): the \"optimizer was never used\" story was WRONG, and eb83ef1e was a codegen NO-OP (reverted 2840b9c5).** Facts: (1) the run path compiles EVERYTHING at cranelift `\"speed\"` already — compile_all_modules_jit (tiered_backend.rs:2261) creates ONE `CraneliftBackend::with_symbols` which hardcodes \"speed\" (cranelift_backend.rs:136-138); tier labels are pure bookkeeping in JIT mode; `mir_opt_level()` has ZERO callers; Baseline's \"none\"/with_fast_compilation has no callers in the run path. (2) MIR O2 (incl. InliningPass) DOES run, once, module-wide, in run_file at main.rs:1676-1698 (\"O2 is now the run-default\"; RAYZOR_OPT_LEVEL / RAYZOR_RAW_MIR override), AFTER tree-shake+stub-link, BEFORE the .mir.cache save (cache replays post-O2 MIR). Plus a per-module O1 in the pipeline (pipeline.rs:518/929/1127). (3) My eb83ef1e \"SIMD→Optimized tier\" changed no codegen AND created a latent interpreted-mode break (Optimized-registered fn bypasses the tier==Baseline compile trigger at 1896-1904 → \"JIT function not found\") — REVERTED; replaced by the real gap fix: **VectorDot was MISSING from function_uses_simd** (tiered_backend.rs:1769-1792; its job = keep SIMD out of the interpreter) — added. (4) Earlier \"1.2→3.3 from the tier change\" was thermal noise + the hdr[] const-lane change; heat-soak swings tok/s ~3x — NEVER attribute wins without cooled alternating A/B.\n\n**THE REAL LLVM-vs-cranelift difference was InliningPass SCHEDULING STARVATION (fixed, disasm-verified).** run_on_module granted ONE inline per block per iteration, processed back-to-front (sort_by_key(Reverse(instruction_index)) + inlined_blocks skip at inlining.rs:793-797, max_iterations=5). q4DotMA4 = ONE ~460-instr block with ~25 accepted trailing sites (splat/sum/getInt32) → the 8 subDotVec sites (InlineHint::Always!) NEVER got a slot. should_inline was fine all along (Always honored at :232). FIX (in inlining.rs): remove the per-block cap; inline ALL accepted sites per caller in strictly DESCENDING instruction_index order (sound because inline_call_site drains only instructions AFTER the call index, so lower-index sites keep valid block+index); call cfg.recompute_predecessors() after mutating a caller (connect_blocks is append-only → stale preds after N same-block splits). Plus kernel: q4DotMA4/q6DotMA4 marked `inline` → Always → inline into the parallelRows closure. RESULT: \u003clambda_0\u003e grew 764→4720 bytes, contains 10 inline `sdot` and ZERO `bl` in the hot path (lldb disasm via haxe_bytes_free breakpoint + JIT map); subDotVec shrank to 96B. Suite 156/156, coherent. Standalone dot copies remain compiled (tree-shake runs BEFORE run_file O2) — dead weight, not called.\n\n**SESSION END STATE (branch haxe-q4q6-matmul-cranelift, 6 commits: 21d13b77 correctness, 54f398a6 extract, 2840b9c5 revert, 5043d39d inliner starvation, d6e55764 arrayAccess typing, 0517fcf6 SpinPool+park prims).** LLVM-verifier regression FIXED via general @:arrayAccess typing (ast_lowering ~13449: Class/Abstract receiver → get()'s return type via find_wrapper_get_method, was Dynamic → boxed i8 as *void → invalid insertelement; + I8/I16 lane widening in hir_to_mir Index fast path). Suite 156/156, both tiers coherent.\n\n**SpinPool (persistent pool) — protocol PROVEN, in-model BLOCKED on Thread.park.** Standalone: bit-correct, 8µs/dispatch (spin) / 419µs (park-idle), torture+shutdown clean. In-model: sleep-idle version is NET SLOWER than serial (2.3 vs 3.3 tok/s — idle workers yield/sleep-contend with the FFI kernels' RUST pool + 1ms wake latency × ~90 matmuls/token). Pool is OPT-IN (RAYZOR_HAXE_MATMUL_POOL=1). park/unpark runtime prims LANDED (rayzor_thread_register_parkable/park/unpark + wrappers/mappings, all inert-safe) but **exposing them as extern Thread.hx members SIGSEGVs GGUF load of UNRELATED modules (deterministic, even FFI path; bisect-proven: Haxe extern member addition alone triggers it — order-dependent cross-module id drift). Park versions saved: /tmp/Thread_park.hx /tmp/SpinPool_park.hx. NEXT SESSION: fix the extern-member id-drift bug, restore park versions, pool should then win.** Also fixed: Linear env gate zero-safe (0/1/2 — duplicated cross-module static starts at 0, old -1 sentinel returned false forever = silent FFI fallback that looked like a 69 tok/s \"win\"); lm_head requant branch must set .pool too (bypasses buildLinear). Loop-spawned closures must capture `this` via explicit `var self = this`.\n\n**Cranelift-vs-LLVM on the SAME Haxe kernel: PARITY** (pre-inline-fix measurement: cranelift 3.657 vs LLVM 3.258 tok/s, 30 tok; cooled A/B post-inline-fix pending). The remaining distance to native FFI (~67-90 tok/s) is ARCHITECTURE of the Haxe path (identical on both tiers): (1) effectively single-threaded (parallelRows spawn-per-call cancels itself; WORKERS=1==10 proven); (2) quantizeQ8K re-runs per matmul though q/k/v (and ffn gate/up) share the same activation row — FFI has x_q8_cache; (3) done: dots now inline. Pool design + hazards in this file below; pool must be INSTANCE-owned (model→Linear→matmul param), NOT a static singleton (baseline-tier static mutation garbage).\n\n**LANDED on branch haxe-q4q6-matmul-cranelift (3 commits): (1) 21d13b77 setFlat + Q6_K kernel; (2) eb83ef1e Optimized-tier for SIMD funcs; (3) 54f398a6 runtime-lane extract rewrite.** Trajectory (thermally noisy): ~1.2 tok/s (Baseline) → ~3.3 (Optimized tier) → ~3.66 (+ extract rewrite). RSS ~1.63GB. Suite 156/156 throughout.\n\n**Extract rewrite (commit 54f398a6) — the alloc-type was the SIGSEGV.** Rewriting SIMD16i8_extract (systems.rs:1407) as spill+indexed-load crashed with `alloc(i8x16)` (vector-typed alloca mis-sizes the slot). Fix: `alloc(I8, count=16)` (raw 16-byte buffer) + vector_store + cast lane i32→i64 (ptr_add assumes i64) + ptr_add(Ptr(I8)) + load(i8) + `(r\u003c\u003c24)\u003e\u003e24`. Now ~3 instr (`str q; ldrsb`) vs the 50-instr 16-extract+15-select chain. This is the GENERAL runtime-lane fix, so it fixed q6DotMA4's 16 `scVec.get(scOff+2*j)` reads/block WITHOUT the planned kernel unroll (unroll now unnecessary). Const-lane gets already fold via `hdr[K]` → hir_to_mir.rs:9373 Index handler → VectorExtract.\n\n**REMAINING levers (not done):** (a) inline q4DotMA4/q6DotMA4 into the parallelRows closure — WON'T fire yet: the closure is non-SIMD → Baseline tier → no InliningPass. Enabler = make function_uses_simd transitive (flag callers-of-SIMD so the closure compiles at Optimized). (b) the general `.get(const)`→VectorExtract method-call fold (hir_to_mir ~13225, mirror 9386) — nicety, q4 already uses `[]`. (c) parallelism (below) — biggest but bad-bet for this laptop.\n\n**The persistent pool is a BAD bet for THIS setup (laptop + cranelift), per the verified design:** it needs a static singleton (garbage on the cranelift Baseline tier — pool code isn't SIMD so my Optimized-tier change doesn't cover it → --llvm-only), workers spin at 100% idle between tokens with NO Haxe `park` primitive (worsens the already-active thermal throttling → risk of NET SLOWER), and needs a mandatory shutdown()+join() or rayzor_wait_all_threads hangs forever at exit. Ceiling is only \"a few-x\" anyway (batch=1 decode = memory-bandwidth-bound, a few cores saturate LPDDR). Do NOT implement without solving the park (idle-backoff ladder) + static-on-cranelift + teardown first.\n\n**ROOT #2: parallelism does not scale.** WORKERS=1 vs WORKERS=10 (adjacent runs, same thermal) are IDENTICAL (~1.2 tok/s). `WorkerPool.parallelRows` (WorkerPool.hx ~131) does `Thread.spawn`+`join` per node PER matmul call — for the small per-token matmuls ([1,512]…[1,8192]) the spawn/join overhead ≈ the parallel benefit, so more threads = net zero. The persistent spin pool (4001079) is RUST/FFI-only. FIX (not done, biggest remaining lever, risky — WorkerPool has a 5-bug history): persistent Haxe worker threads reused across parallelRows calls (spin-wait dispatch), NOT spawn-per-call.\n\nTwo earlier levers:\n1. **const-lane SIMD extract fold — LANDED (partial), measured win.** `hdr.get(K)` (method call) compiles to the 50-instr `SIMD16i8_extract` wrapper (16 vector_extracts + 15-deep runtime select). But `hdr[K]` (array-access) with a CONSTANT literal K routes through hir_to_mir.rs:9373 `HirExprKind::Index` → direct `VectorExtract` (single lane read). Changed Q4Matmul.hx `hdr.get(N)`→`hdr[N]` (q4 header, 16 const lanes) + q6 `dVec[14/15]`: sustained **~3.3 tok/s** (from ~1.1 short-run), coherent. NOTE the fold only fires for CONST literal lanes; q6's `scVec.get(scOff+2*j)` is a RUNTIME lane → still the wrapper (needs n/j unroll, or a general `get(const)`→VectorExtract fold in the Call path mirroring the Index path).\n2. **thread-spawn per call (~17% + likely the memory)** — persistent Haxe worker pool not yet built.\n\nMemory: 1.59GB peak on a 30-tok run (model ~1GB + overhead); user reports up to 3GB (grows with context / possible tensor-return leak — InsertFreePass doesn't free extern tensor returns). Target ≤1GB. FFI path (default, no HAXE_MATMUL) ~67 tok/s, \u003c1GB — native Rust + the persistent spin pool.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_cranelift_sdot_and_haxe_q4_status` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}